<h1>Video 086 — join(), sleep(), and thread interruption</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-6 || join() || sleep() || Thread Interruption</p>
<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>This lecture continues the multithreading series (Part 6). It covers three major areas:</p>
<ol><li>`join()` loopholes — child waiting for main, mutual join deadlock, self-join deadlock</li></ol>
<ol><li>`sleep()` — purpose, signatures, lifecycle impact, slide-rotator demo</li></ol>
<ol><li>Thread interruption — interrupt(), deferred interrupt behavior, comparison of yield / join / sleep</li></ol>
<p>ASR glossary (decode Whisper errors):</p>
<h3>00:10 — Recap: main waiting for child (Case 1 review)</h3>
<p>In the previous example (Part 5), the main thread called t.join() and had to wait until the child thread completed its work.</p>
<p>Case 2 (new): Reverse the scenario — the child thread must wait until the main thread completes.</p>
<p>Waiting of child thread until completing main thread.</p>
<p>Terminology matters: which thread must call join(), and on which thread object?</p>
<h3>01:17 — Case 2 setup: child calls join on main</h3>
<p>Problem: Inside MyThread.run(), the child thread must call join() on the main thread object. But the child does not have a reference to the main thread by default.</p>
<p>Twist: How can the child get the main thread reference?</p>
<p>Solution: Use a static `Thread` variable in MyThread and assign it from main before starting the child.</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    static Thread mt;  // will hold main thread reference

    public void run() {
        // child will call join on main via mt
    }
}</pre>
<p>Key insight: run() is executed by the child thread, not main. The child needs access to the main thread object to call mt.join().</p>
<h3>02:38 — MyThread.run() with mt.join()</h3>
<p>Inside run(), the child thread calls join() on the main thread object stored in mt:</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    static Thread mt;

    public void run() {
        try {
            mt.join();  // child waits until main completes
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            // handle
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Flow after `t.start()`:</p>
<h3>03:55 — ThreadJoinDemo1: assigning main reference</h3>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadJoinDemo1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        MyThread.mt = Thread.currentThread();  // mt → main thread

        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Main Thread&quot;);
            Thread.sleep(2000);  // main sleeps 2 seconds each iteration
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Why `MyThread.mt = Thread.currentThread()`?</p>
<ul><li>Thread.currentThread() returns the currently executing thread — here, the main thread</li></ul>
<ul><li>mt is static, so it is accessible as MyThread.mt from main</li></ul>
<ul><li>After this assignment, mt points to the main thread object for the child to use in run()</li></ul>
<p>Thread count after `t.start()`: 2 threads — main and child.</p>
<h3>05:26 — Main thread loop with sleep</h3>
<p>Main continues its job while child waits:</p>
<pre class='code'>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(&quot;Main Thread&quot;);
    Thread.sleep(2000);
}</pre>
<p>Main uses Thread.sleep(2000) — must handle InterruptedException (here via throws on main).</p>
<h3>06:32 — Child enters waiting via mt.join()</h3>
<p>try {
    mt.join();  // child thread calls join on main thread object
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
}</p>
<p>Semantics:</p>
<ul><li>Executed by: child thread</li></ul>
<ul><li>Called on: main thread object (mt)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Effect: child enters waiting state until main thread completes (finishes main method)</li></ul>
<p>After main completes → child resumes → prints &quot;Child Thread&quot; 10 times.</p>
<h3>08:13 — Case 2 expected output</h3>
<p>Output order:</p>
<p>Main Thread
Main Thread
... (10 times)
Child Thread
Child Thread
... (10 times)</p>
<p>Rule: Child cannot print until main finishes, because mt.join() blocks the child.</p>
<p>Main can wait for child. Child can wait for main. Both are possible via `join()`.</p>
<h3>09:56 — Live demo: ThreadJoinDemo1</h3>
<p>Durga Sir runs ThreadJoinDemo1.java:</p>
<ul><li>Main thread prints repeatedly (with 2-second pauses)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child thread waits silently</li></ul>
<ul><li>After main completes, child prints 10 times</li></ul>
<ul><li>Same output even on a slow machine — child must wait</li></ul>
<p>Theory note for notes:</p>
<p>In the above example, child thread calls `join()` on main thread object. Hence, child thread has to wait until completing main thread.</p>
<h3>15:15 — Case 2 output summary</h3>
<p>Main Thread .......... (×10)
Child Thread ......... (×10)</p>
<h3>15:52 — Case 3: mutual join → deadlock</h3>
<p>Scenario: Both threads call join() on each other.</p>
<pre class='code'>// After t.start():
t.join();        // main waits for child  — executed by MAIN
// Inside child run():
mt.join();       // child waits for main — executed by CHILD</pre>
<p>After `t.start()` — 2 threads:</p>
<p>Result: Deadlock — both wait forever for each other.</p>
<h3>17:10 — Case 3 code structure</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    static Thread mt;

    public void run() {
        try {
            mt.join();  // child waits for main
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class ThreadJoinDemo3 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        MyThread.mt = Thread.currentThread();
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        t.join();  // main waits for child — DEADLOCK with mt.join()
    }
}</pre>
<p>Behavior:</p>
<ul><li>Code compiles fine</li></ul>
<ul><li>Program runs but cursor blinks forever</li></ul>
<ul><li>Neither thread can proceed — classic deadlock</li></ul>
<p>If main thread calls join() on child, and child calls join() on main thread object, both threads wait forever. Program stuck — deadlock.</p>
<h3>19:54 — Case 4: self-join deadlock</h3>
<p>Scenario: A thread calls join() on itself.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.currentThread().join();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Analysis:</p>
<p>Paradox: Main must complete to satisfy join(), but it cannot complete without passing the join() line → infinite wait.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Equivalent logic:
// main thread calls join on main thread itself
// → main enters waiting until main completes
// → impossible → program stuck (deadlock)</pre>
<p>Demo: Test.java compiles; on run, cursor blinks forever.</p>
<p>If a thread calls join() on the same thread itself, the program will be stuck (deadlock). Thread waits infinite amount of time.</p>
<h3>25:08 — Case 4 theory summary</h3>
<p>Four join loopholes covered:</p>
<h3>25:45 — Introduction to sleep()</h3>
<p>Next method: Thread.sleep()</p>
<p>Human analogy: When tired, you rest. For threads: when a thread should not perform any operation for a particular amount of time, use `sleep()`.</p>
<p>Thread requirement:</p>
<p>If a thread does not want to perform any operation for a particular amount of time → use sleep method.</p>
<p>(Durga Sir&#x27;s motivational aside: control sleeping habits; Proverbs — &quot;sleep makes man poor&quot;; max ~6 hours sleep recommended.)</p>
<h3>33:12 — Real-world sleep examples</h3>
<p>1. PowerPoint slide rotator</p>
<ul><li>One thread rotates slides</li></ul>
<ul><li>After each slide → sleep 2 minutes → next slide auto-advances</li></ul>
<p>2. Blinking bulb (GUI)</p>
<ul><li>Thread displays bulb ON</li></ul>
<ul><li>Sleep 1 second</li></ul>
<ul><li>OFF, sleep, ON — blinking effect</li></ul>
<p>Pattern: Wherever pausing for a specific duration is required programmatically → `Thread.sleep()`.</p>
<h3>36:37 — sleep() method signatures</h3>
<p>Two overloaded `sleep` methods (unlike join which has three):</p>
<pre class='code'>public static native void sleep(long millis) throws InterruptedException;

public static void sleep(long millis, int nanos) throws InterruptedException;</pre>
<p>Why only 2 sleep methods, not 3?</p>
<ul><li>join() has a no-argument form: wait forever until target completes</li></ul>
<ul><li>sleep() always requires a time period — &quot;sleep forever without time&quot; is meaningless</li></ul>
<ul><li>You must specify: 6 seconds, 6 hours, etc.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// NO such method exists:
// Thread.sleep();  // forever — NOT in API</pre>
<h3>40:11 — InterruptedException on every sleep()</h3>
<p>Every sleep method throws `InterruptedException` — a checked exception.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Must handle via try-catch OR throws
try {
    Thread.sleep(2000);
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    // handle interruption
}</pre>
<p>Or:</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
    Thread.sleep(2000);
}</pre>
<p>Otherwise: compile-time error.</p>
<p>Whenever we use sleep method, compulsory we should handle InterruptedException either by try-catch or by throws. Otherwise compile-time error.</p>
<h3>43:46 — sleep() impact on thread lifecycle</h3>
<p>Normal lifecycle:</p>
<p>NEW/BORN → start() → READY/RUNNABLE → scheduler assigns CPU → RUNNING
    → run() completes → DEAD</p>
<p>When running thread calls sleep:</p>
<p>Thread.sleep(1000);                    // overload 1
Thread.sleep(1000, 100);               // overload 2 — millis + nanos</p>
<p>Running thread → enters sleeping state (similar to waiting).</p>
<p>When does sleeping thread wake up?</p>
<p>After either → thread returns to READY/RUNNABLE → scheduler may assign CPU → RUNNING → eventually DEAD.</p>
<p>RUNNING --sleep()--&gt; SLEEPING --time expires OR interrupt--&gt; READY/RUNNABLE --&gt; RUNNING --&gt; DEAD</p>
<h3>49:44 — SlideRotator example</h3>
<pre class='code'>class SlideRotator {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Slide &quot; + i);
            Thread.sleep(5000);  // 5 second pause between slides
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Output behavior:</p>
<ul><li>Slide 1 displayed</li></ul>
<ul><li>Wait 5 seconds</li></ul>
<ul><li>Slide 2 displayed</li></ul>
<ul><li>... up to Slide 10</li></ul>
<p>Use cases: PPT auto-advance, photo album slideshow (configure 20s, 30s per image).</p>
<h3>51:36 — SlideRotator live demo</h3>
<p>Durga Sir compiles and runs SlideRotator.java:</p>
<ul><li>Code compiles fine</li></ul>
<ul><li>Each slide appears after 5-second gap</li></ul>
<ul><li>Duration configurable per requirement</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Photo album variant — same pattern
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= photoCount; i++) {
    System.out.println(&quot;Photo &quot; + i);
    Thread.sleep(20000);  // 20 seconds per photo
}</pre>
<h3>54:33 — Bridge to thread interruption</h3>
<p>While covering join() and sleep(), a recurring theme appears:</p>
<ul><li>A waiting thread (due to join) may be interrupted</li></ul>
<ul><li>A sleeping thread may be interrupted</li></ul>
<p>Question: How can one thread interrupt another? Is interruption good or bad?</p>
<p>Depends on situation — mostly considered bad, but sometimes required.</p>
<h3>55:53 — Interruption analogy: missing the last bus</h3>
<p>Story: Traveling to native place (~50 km). Last bus at 11:30 PM. You ask fellow villagers: &quot;I&#x27;m sleeping; wake me when the bus comes.&quot;</p>
<p>They forget → you miss the bus → wait hours for next day.</p>
<p>Lesson: Sometimes interruption is required — not always bad.</p>
<h3>57:51 — interrupt() method</h3>
<p>How one thread interrupts another:</p>
<pre class='code'>public void interrupt()</pre>
<p>Thread class provides interrupt() — instance method (not static).</p>
<p>Targets:</p>
<p>A thread can interrupt a sleeping or waiting thread using `interrupt()` method of Thread class.</p>
<h3>1:00:06 — MyThread lazy thread (interrupt demo part 1)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
                System.out.println(&quot;I am lazy thread&quot;);
                Thread.sleep(2000);  // sleeps 2 seconds each iteration
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;I got interrupted&quot;);
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Without interruption: Loop runs 10 times, printing + sleeping each time.</p>
<h3>1:02:15 — ThreadInterruptDemo</h3>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadInterruptDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        t.interrupt();  // main interrupts child — LINE 1
        System.out.println(&quot;End of main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Execution analysis:</p>
<p>Output (with `t.interrupt()` active):</p>
<p>I am lazy thread
I got interrupted
End of main</p>
<p>Child loop: Runs only 1 time (not 10), because interrupt during sleep throws exception → catch block → run ends.</p>
<h3>1:04:49 — Commenting out t.interrupt()</h3>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadInterruptDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        // t.interrupt();  // COMMENTED — no interruption
        System.out.println(&quot;End of main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<ul><li>&quot;I am lazy thread&quot; prints 10 times</li></ul>
<ul><li>Catch block not executed</li></ul>
<ul><li>InterruptedException never raised</li></ul>
<h3>1:06:41 — Re-enable interrupt: confirmed behavior</h3>
<p>With comment removed:</p>
<p>I am lazy thread
I got interrupted
End of main</p>
<p>Summary:</p>
<p>Who interrupts whom? Main thread interrupts child thread.</p>
<h3>1:12:20 — Critical loophole: interrupt when target NOT sleeping/waiting</h3>
<p>Question: Main calls t.interrupt() but child is not in sleeping or waiting state — child is actively running (e.g. long loop). What happens?</p>
<p>Options considered:</p>
<p>Answer: Interrupt call behaves like a snake (movie Swetha Nagu analogy) — it waits until the target enters sleeping or waiting state, then strikes.</p>
<p>If target thread is not in sleeping/waiting state when interrupt() is called → no immediate impact. Interrupt call is queued/waiting until target enters sleeping or waiting state.</p>
<h3>1:17:48 — Deferred interrupt demo: 10,000 iteration loop</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;I am lazy thread &quot; + i);
        }
        try {
            System.out.println(&quot;I want to sleep&quot;);
            Thread.sleep(10000);  // 10 seconds
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;I got interrupted&quot;);
        }
    }
}
class ThreadSleepDemo1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        t.interrupt();  // called while child still in for-loop
    }
}</pre>
<p>Timeline:</p>
<ol><li>Child starts 10,000-iteration loop (NOT sleeping yet)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Main immediately calls t.interrupt()</li></ol>
<ol><li>No immediate effect — JVM holds the interrupt request</li></ol>
<ol><li>Main continues (does not wait)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child completes all 10,000 iterations</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child tries Thread.sleep(10000)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Immediately — pending interrupt fires → InterruptedException → &quot;I got interrupted&quot;</li></ol>
<p>Key points:</p>
<ul><li>Main thread does not wait — JVM handles deferred interrupt</li></ul>
<ul><li>Interrupt call never wasted (unless target dies without ever sleeping/waiting)</li></ul>
<h3>1:21:51 — Demo without interrupt (commented)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadSleepDemo1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        // t.interrupt();  // commented
    }
}</pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<ul><li>10,000 &quot;I am lazy thread&quot; lines print</li></ul>
<ul><li>&quot;I want to sleep&quot; prints</li></ul>
<ul><li>Sleeps full 10 seconds</li></ul>
<ul><li>No &quot;I got interrupted&quot;</li></ul>
<ul><li>Program ends normally</li></ul>
<h3>1:23:04 — Demo with interrupt enabled</h3>
<p>Uncomment t.interrupt():</p>
<ul><li>10,000 loop iterations complete first</li></ul>
<ul><li>&quot;I want to sleep&quot; appears</li></ul>
<ul><li>Immediately &quot;I got interrupted&quot; — no 10-second sleep</li></ul>
<ul><li>Pending interrupt consumed at first sleep/wait opportunity</li></ul>
<h3>1:26:08 — Star note: deferred interrupt rules</h3>
<p>⭐ If you call `interrupt()` and target thread is NOT in sleeping/waiting state:</p>
<p>- No immediate impact</p>
<p>- Interrupt call will wait until target thread enters sleeping or waiting state</p>
<p>- Once target enters sleeping/waiting → interrupt fires → InterruptedException</p>
<p>Analogy: Snake follows heroine everywhere until it gets a chance to bite.</p>
<h3>1:29:57 — When interrupt call IS wasted</h3>
<p>Edge case: What if target thread dies before ever entering sleeping or waiting?</p>
<p>Example: Heroine dies in plane crash before snake gets chance to bite → snake has no target.</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;I am lazy thread &quot; + i);
        }
        // NO sleep(), NO join() — thread ends without ever waiting/sleeping
    }
}</pre>
<p>MyThread t = new MyThread();
t.start();
t.interrupt();  // child busy in loop, then dies — never sleeps
// interrupt call WASTED — only case where it has no effect</p>
<p>Only case where interrupt call is wasted: Target thread never enters sleeping or waiting state in its entire lifetime, then terminates. If target dies without sleeping/waiting → interrupt has no impact.</p>
<h3>1:31:49 — Theory: interrupt call waiting (10,000 loop example)</h3>
<p>In the above example, interrupt call waited until child thread completes for loop 10,000 times. Once child entered sleeping state → interrupt immediately fired.</p>
<h3>1:36:10 — Comparison table: yield vs join vs sleep</h3>
<p>Durga Sir summarizes all three methods with a comparison table.</p>
<h4>Purpose</h4>
<pre class='code'>// yield — give chance to same-priority threads
Thread.yield();

// join — wait for another thread to finish
t.join();
t.join(5000);           // wait max 5 seconds
t.join(5000, 0);        // millis + nanos

// sleep — pause this thread
Thread.sleep(3000);
Thread.sleep(3000, 500000);  // millis + nanos</pre>
<h4>Overloaded?</h4>
<h4>final?</h4>
<h4>Throws InterruptedException?</h4>
<pre class='code'>// join and sleep require handling
try {
    t.join();
    Thread.sleep(1000);
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
}</pre>
<h4>Native?</h4>
<h4>Static?</h4>
<pre class='code'>// Static vs instance
Thread.yield();           // static
Thread.sleep(1000);       // static
t.join();                 // instance — called on Thread object</pre>
<p>Important exam point: You cannot replace one method with another — each has distinct purpose.</p>
<h3>1:38:42 — Exam FAQ from comparison</h3>
<p>Common interview questions from this table:</p>
<ul><li>What is the purpose of yield / join / sleep?</li></ul>
<ul><li>Difference between yield and join?</li></ul>
<ul><li>Difference between join and sleep?</li></ul>
<ul><li>Is join overloaded? Is sleep overloaded?</li></ul>
<ul><li>Which methods are final? Which throw InterruptedException?</li></ul>
<ul><li>Which are native? Which are static?</li></ul>
