<h2>Quick reference — join() signatures (from prior video, used here)</h2>
<pre class='code'>public final void join() throws InterruptedException;

public final void join(long millis) throws InterruptedException;

public final void join(long millis, int nanos) throws InterruptedException;</pre>
<p>All three are final instance methods — called on a Thread reference:</p>
<p>t.join();              // wait forever
t.join(5000);          // wait up to 5 seconds
t.join(5000, 0);       // wait up to 5 seconds + 0 nanos</p>
<h2>Quick reference — sleep() signatures</h2>
<pre class='code'>public static native void sleep(long millis) throws InterruptedException;

public static void sleep(long millis, int nanos) throws InterruptedException;</pre>
<h2>Quick reference — interrupt()</h2>
<pre class='code'>public void interrupt()</pre>
<ul><li>Interrupts sleeping or waiting threads</li></ul>
<ul><li>If target not sleeping/waiting → interrupt waits (deferred)</li></ul>
<ul><li>If target never sleeps/waits in lifetime → interrupt wasted</li></ul>
<h2>Complete runnable examples index</h2>
<h2>Exam traps (OCJP/SCJP style)</h2>
<ol><li>Child joining main requires a way to get main thread reference (Thread.currentThread() stored in static variable).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Mutual join = deadlock; self join = deadlock.</li></ol>
<ol><li>sleep() always needs time argument — no forever-sleep overload.</li></ol>
<ol><li>All sleep methods throw checked InterruptedException.</li></ol>
<ol><li>interrupt() on running thread → deferred until sleep/wait.</li></ol>
<ol><li>interrupt() wasted only if target never enters sleep/wait before dying.</li></ol>
<ol><li>join is instance + final; sleep/yield are static.</li></ol>
<ol><li>sleep(long) is native; sleep(long, int) is not.</li></ol>
